  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low pressure lifting through the region this morning is\nbringing locally heavy showers resulting in flooding in some\nplaces. These showers will taper off through the late morning\ninto the early afternoon as the low departs. \n\nThe low and associated warm front is slowly lifting through \nNew Jersey this morning. With the low centered over south Jersey\nas of mid morning, the heaviest rain is focused north of the low\nover parts of eastern PA into mainly central and northern NJ.\nThe low will continue to track northward over the next several\nhours reaching Long Island early this afternoon. As this occurs,\nshowers will taper off over the area from southwest to\nnortheast. In fact from areas around Philly and points to the\nwest and northwest the rain rates have diminished considerably\nover the past hour or so. That said, expect there to continue to\nbe lingering flooding issues until the water works its way\nthrough the system as many areas have seen 1-2+ inches of rain\nin just a couple hours. \n\nOnce the rain is completely out of the area by around 2 PM, the\nremainder of the day will be dry with some partial clearing\ndeveloping. A weak cold front swings through this afternoon \nbehind the low followed by a secondary cold front late \ntonight/early Monday Morning. Some light rain showers will be \npossible with the secondary cold front, but with dry air already\nfiltering in to the area by then, do not expect much more than \nlight rain showers.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front Thursday into Friday may bring some \nshowers and thunderstorms. \n\nAn upper-level trough across central into eastern Canada is forecast \nto push a cold front into our area at the end of next week. While \nthe guidance, including ensembles, are in decent agreement showing \nthis trough, there are significant differences with the \namplification and therefore speed of the trough. This will have \nimpacts to the cold front itself. If the main energy slides far \nenough north of our area, the front may tend to be slower moving. \nThis scenario would open the door for a greater chance for a round \nof convection. A faster movement to the front would lessen the \nlength of convection over our area. The front may then stall to our \nsouth into next weekend, with one or more possible waves of low \npressure tracking along it with time. A plume of deeper moisture \nlooks to get drawn northward ahead of this front, therefore some \nheavy rain potential may exist with it and this also depends on \nwhether it stalls or not. Confidence remains low during this time \nframe.",
